# Third-party libraries are latest stable only

The factory ships to Google Play. Play flags outdated SDKs in the bundle, and an antique
transitive is still a shipped SDK even when the app never imports it. The owner's canon:
use only the modern stack; third-party libraries must be the **latest stable**; do not leave
an APK on what a Google Maven POM asked for years ago when a constraint can raise it.

## Measured instance

On 2026-10-05 Play notified that Mark Book for Teachers (`teacher-gradebook`) shipped
`androidx.fragment:fragment:1.1.0`. Gradle showed the path:

```text
androidx.fragment:fragment:1.1.0
\--- com.google.android.gms:play-services-basement:18.9.0
     \--- com.android.billingclient:billing:9.1.0
          \--- billing-ktx → com.gexetr.capability:billing → app
```

Billing **9.1.0 was already the latest** stable. The app is Compose-only and does not use
Fragment UI. GMS basement still requested Fragment 1.1.0. The fix is a dependency
**constraint** to Fragment **1.9.1** (latest stable measured that day), not a Billing
downgrade and not excluding GMS (Billing needs it).

## Rules

- **Third-party coordinates on the classpath are latest stable.** Prefer mainstream libraries.
  When a transitive is antique and Play-flagged, **constrain it up** — do not ship the 2019
  request GMS encoded in its POM.
- **Known Play-outdated coordinates must not ship.** The measured instance is
  `androidx.fragment:fragment:1.1.0`. A slot that resolves Fragment below the factory floor
  (`1.9.1`, `FRAGMENT_FLOOR` in `scripts/core/deps.mjs`) fails the gate.
- **Billing stays on current Play Billing.** Do not downgrade `com.android.billingclient`
  to escape the Fragment notice. Pin Fragment instead.
- **When Billing is present, constrain Fragment.** Every living path that puts Billing on the
  classpath — `capabilities/billing`, and every slot `app/build.gradle.kts` that
  `implementation`s `capability.billing` / `billing-ktx` — carries:
  `constraints { api("androidx.fragment:fragment:<floor>") { because("…") } }`
  (catalog version ref is fine). Document in a one-line comment that Compose apps do not use
  Fragment; the constraint only upgrades the transitive so Play stops flagging 1.1.0.
- **Do not add Fragment as an app feature.** No `implementation(libs.androidx.fragment)` for
  UI, no Fragment navigation, no Fragment-hosted screens. Constraint only. Compose owns the UI.

## How it is verified

- `build.fragmentConstraint` / `deps.fixtures` in `scripts/core/deps.mjs`, registered in
  `scripts/core/index.mjs`: a billing-enabled slot (or a build that depends on billing) without
  a Fragment constraint at or above the floor FAILs; fixtures prove the check can fail.
- Observed resolution: `./gradlew :app:dependencyInsight --dependency androidx.fragment:fragment
  --configuration releaseRuntimeClasspath` must select the floor, not 1.1.0.

## The honest limit

The gate reads **source** (`app/build.gradle.kts` and the version catalog). It cannot prove the
resolved APK graph without Gradle. After changing the constraint, republish
`capabilities/` to mavenLocal and re-run `dependencyInsight` on a billing slot before calling
the Play notice closed.
